the changing of the guard at the Tomb of  the Unknown Soldier in Arlington  National Cemetery is one of the many  roles of the Army's old guard the unit  conducts more than 6,000 ceremonies each  year including funeral escorts parades  and presidential events former old guard  platoon leader Senator Tom cotton gives  us an inside look at the Army's oldest  active duty regiment in his new book  sacred duty a soldiers tour at Arlington  National Cemetery senator cotton good  morning good to have you here we're  gonna talk about that the old guard in a  bit but first happy birthday thank you  42 42 starting to get up there it's a  crazy man at the table I will say this  you know I spent much of the last year  back at Arlington National Cemetery  researching sacred duty with the  soldiers of the old guard and a lot of  those soldiers to include those tomb  sentinels or 19 or 20 years old a little  old when I'm back at the cemetery Fort  Myer with the soldiers of the old guard  senator I want to ask you about the  trade negotiations that are going on  right now there was that just recently a  University of Arkansas study from your  home state that said retaliatory tariffs  by China could end up hurting many  farmers in your state is President Trump  hurting the Americans that he promised  to help so these tariffs are gonna end  up hurting both the Chinese and some  Americans I'll grant you that  I think the ultimately hurt the Chinese  more than they will Americans in part  because Chinese companies and their  government have been cheating the United  States for so long there will be some  sacrifice on the part of Americans I  grant you that but I also would say that  sacrifice is pretty minimal compared to  the sacrifices that our soldiers make  overseas that our fallen heroes or laid  to rest in Arlington make that are right  about here those two sacrifices that's  rice and the way when I'm home in  Arkansas hear from farmers who are  worried about opening up new markets and  getting their products to market but  they also understand that China is a  serious competitor of the United States  and wants to displace us around the  world and they look at the sacrifices  that soldiers sailors airmen and Marines  make around the world and they are  willing to bear some of those sacrifices  in the short term hopefully in the long  term ensure our long-term prosperity and  security why not tell them now in the  front  and why not tell them this sacrifice and  the higher prices you'll receive you  know you're gonna have to pay or a part  of this conflict as opposed to saying  what's not true which is that China's  gonna pay these tariffs to the Treasury  well China will ultimately be paying a  price for these because Americans may be  shifting their consumption or their  investment decisions away from Chinese  products that have oftentimes been  dumped in the United States you know we  have our Kansans who are benefitting  from these tariffs as well you know with  eastern Arkansas we have one of the  largest steel production areas now in  the country they obviously are doing  very well again I'm not gonna say that  no one is going to make a sacrifice but  in the long term the effort is to make  sure that the United States remains  preeminent as a global superpower both  in the economic and the security since  it seems to translate between 700 to  $1,000 for the average American family  which is a lot for many families is it  worth that cost if we remain the world's  largest economy and the world's dominant  superpower in the short term I say this  worth that cost and what I hear from  most are kanzan is that they are willing  to bear a short-term price not be  straight with that because you know you  have obviously Walmart matters a lot in  your stage largest retailer in the world  and they say about this hike they says  it's going to create a serious burden on  families it's just a question of you  know people don't like politicians  because sometimes they don't tell them  what's coming and have they really been  you say that and yeah I don't know many  farmers or ranchers in Arkansas who were  confused about what was going to happen  here I mean they obviously they they  have from the very beginning you know  from the minute you know 200 years ago  we started farming land in Arkansas knew  that overseas markets were vital to  their prosperity and to have new markets  sell their crops or their products but  ultimately they also know that we cannot  let China continue to cheat as they have  been for 30 years well we've talked to  farmers who seem to disagree with that  and I'm curious about your definition of  short-term what does that mean short  term  I hope sooner rather than later you know  the president said that we were close to  having an agreement within the last week  or two and that China started trying to  renegotiate the terms of the deals they  often happen time I think it's the right  thing to do is to hold China to their in  their the end of their bargain and not  give them an easy deal I think a lot a  lot of people who are criticizing the  tariff snail would be criticizing the  president  if he had given if he had struck a bad  deal with China last week let me ask you  about sacred duty it's an extraordinary  service that these soldiers provide you  were a part of that what was the hardest  part of that training and and and being  in that part of the service they're  performing funerals in the cemetery is  pressure-packed job is also very  humbling lieutenant colonel Allen Keogh  is the battalion commander for the  battalion that has primary  responsibility for the cemetery he is a  veteran of the elite 75th Ranger  Regiment he served five overseas combat  doors and he said about his time in  Arlington I've never felt pressure like  this anywhere in the army and that's  because even though we might perform  twenty to thirty funerals a day you know  over a hundred a week for that family  that is a once-in-a-lifetime moment that  is truly a no-fail zero defect mission  give people some sense of the amazing  discipline for the smallest little  detail so as I described in sacred duty  soldiers spend hundreds of hours  learning their craft which is different  from most of what the army does from  preparing their uniforms pressing them  building their medals shining their  shoes all those details though are not  just for show or for fussing us there or  to show that last in Della's indelible  image of Honor to the family and I think  most readers of sacred duty would be  able to take away an inspiring story of  excellence and commitment to one's craft  whether it's in the workplace or in  one's church or coaching a little league  team that's an inspiring ethos that  these soldiers in the old guard show for  our nation's fallen that I think any of  us can apply to our day to day life the  time the care and the respect that goes  into it is really remarkable and you  have one line that says history lives  inside Arlington gates but the old guard  never takes a break from history every  single day funerals are happening at but  every single day history is being made  as well at literally the day I turned in  the manuscript for this book is the day  President George HW Bush passed away so  for the next six days I lived that  experience with the old guard kind of in  real time and as I write about in the  conclusion of sacred duty that's one of  the rarest events they perform it's also  one of the highest stakes that's why  when I was there just like today the old  guard will train with its sister  services regularly to be prepared for a  state funeral because you never know  when something like that might happen  having  stood up close from the side of a grave  it is the most amazing thing and it's  such a testament and so glad you wrote  this book Thank You senator cough thank  you sacred duty as soldiers tour at  Arlington National Cemetery goes on sale  tomorrow wherever you like to buy books
 

